# `Uint16Array.from(bytes)` let `bytes` go in Link, and with it every method of that view anywhere in the program. from's row reads the own values of its items, and a row that does so asks whether the
# instance is as its class makes it IN STORAGE too: whether something may lie under an index above it. That was asked of the prototypes of views as it is of the prototypes of lists, and they do not close
# their index keys. A read under an index never climbs from a view: behind the end it gives undefined. So nothing lies under an index above a view, whatever its prototypes hold.
# withOwnIterator is WRITTEN ON: an iterator of its own under the symbol: from runs it, the view is let go and its slice is not found.
# f1 = copyOfPart, f3 = copyOfOther
# RUN: walk f1:Body f3:Body mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK-NOT: PropGet {{*}} key="slice"
# CHECK: Uint8Array @JSValue = CallBuiltin {{*}} overload=TypedArrayPrototypeSlice/0
# CHECK-LABEL: code f3:Body
# CHECK-NOT: overload=TypedArrayPrototypeSlice
# RUN(SHELL): run-in-shell globalThis.sink = print;
# SHELL: 42 | 360 | 2 | 0,0,0,0 | 42 | 360 | 2 | 5,6
